WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.779
ROSETT: And now it's my pleasure to make a
brief introduction.

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:11.670
You have the biography, but part of the international
drama of Iran has turned on matters of money,

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.810
finance, trade, and sanctions.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:21.360
And in this drama, one of the important actors
has been Dr. Valiollah Seif.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.210
Born and educated in Iran, he has spent much
of his career in banking, running at least

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:31.760
half a dozen institutions including Bank Melli,
Bank Mellat, and Future Bank in Bahrain.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.050
In 2013, he was appointed as governor of the
Central Bank of the Islamic Republic of Iran

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.610
and chairman of the Money and Credit Council.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:44.610
And without further delay, I'm pleased to
introduce him.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.490
(Applause.)

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:57.800
SEIF: Good morning, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:08.020
I am delighted to be here today and have this
discussion to the sidelines of the IMF-World

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:10.719
Bank spring meetings.

00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:20.639
Allow me first to thank the Council on Foreign
Relation(s) for the excellent arrangement

00:01:20.639 --> 00:01:23.280
made for this meeting.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:31.759
I will briefly present an overview of recent
economic and financial development in Iran,

00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:43.229
the progress made in strengthening the resilience
of our economy, including the banking and

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:53.039
also-and our expectation for better economic
ties with our partners in light of the significant

00:01:53.039 --> 00:01:58.729
trade and investment opportunities our country
offers.

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:07.219
I will also highlight the challenges we face
in this post-sanctions environment which we

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:10.690
need to address to move forward.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:22.599
As you know, following the intensified negotiations
between Iran and the P5+1 that entered into

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:33.500
effect in January 2016 of the so-called Joint
Comprehensive Plan of Action-JCPOA-has opened

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:42.849
a new era for the Iranian economy and its
relations with the rest of the world.

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:53.200
On our side, we were working not only to address
the negative effects of the sanctions by making

00:02:53.200 --> 00:03:02.239
the economy more resilient, but also to ensure
that it will be in a better position to respond

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:10.019
to increased trade and investment once the
sanctions are lifted.

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:18.470
Over the past two years, we have made significant
progress in restoring macroeconomic stability,

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:27.159
improving fiscal management, strengthening
the banking system, stabilizing FX market,

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:31.249
and advancing growth, enhancing structural
reforms.

00:03:31.249 --> 00:03:39.560
We achieved all of this during a period when
the unilateral sanctions continued.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:54.580
This also evidences the fact that, contrary
to baseless allegation that some people made,

00:03:54.580 --> 00:04:06.329
sanctions did not and could not force to engage
into a negotiation with our P5+1 colleague

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:17.220
or compromise our legitimate rights to continue
our peaceful nuclear activities.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:24.990
As noted, the outcome of these efforts is
very positive.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:38.750
Even though growth stagnated in 2015-16, reflecting
the sharp decline in oil prices, and a wait-and-see

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:47.509
attitude on the part of consumer and investors,
it is expected to rebound to about 5 percent

00:04:47.509 --> 00:04:51.750
in 2016-17.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:05:01.199
Inflation has dropped to below 12 percent,
and is projected to decline further to single

00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:04.880
digit by end 2017-2016-17.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:17.070
As the central bank tightened money to the
policy and enhanced its capability-its credibility,

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:25.169
the foreign-exchange market has stabilized,
and the steps are being taken to unify the

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:32.620
exchange rate once the normal account relations
with foreign banks are adequately restored.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:43.270
Let me now turn to the Iranian banking sector,
which has a critical role to play, not only

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:51.800
in the Iranian economy but also in facilitating
a successful transition to a more dynamic

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.900
engagement with the rest of the world.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:06:04.050
With a history of nine decades, and extensive
correspondent and account relationship with

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:14.560
many Asian and European banks in recent decades,
and even with the U.S. banks prior to 1995,

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:20.759
our banking system can draw on its rich experience
and expertise.

00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:27.789
This does not mean that our banking system
does not face many challenges.

00:06:27.789 --> 00:06:32.629
It does, and we are addressing them.

00:06:32.629 --> 00:06:41.330
State-owned as well as private banks are being
restructured and recapitalized to make them

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:46.699
more robust financially.

00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:58.379
Shadow credit institutions are obliged to
consolidate and apply for a banking license.

00:06:58.379 --> 00:07:08.680
NPLs are being monitored closely, and additional
steps will be taken to repay and strengthen

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.470
bank balance sheets.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:18.879
We are addressing these problems with assistance
from the International Monetary Fund, and

00:07:18.879 --> 00:07:26.970
we are also strengthening the central bank's
supervisory powers, and extending them to

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:30.840
all banking and credit institutions.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:40.610
Iranian banks overseas are subject to stringent
supervision by home and host supervisors.

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:49.569
While reduction in international financial
transactions between Iran and foreign banks

00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:57.729
has adversely impacted service delivery, the
central bank is strengthening regulation and

00:07:57.729 --> 00:08:06.729
supervision to ensure proper risk-management
practices in banks and compliance with international

00:08:06.729 --> 00:08:13.500
standards, including on corporate governance,
anti-money laundering, and capital and liquidity

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.050
requirements set by the Basel Committee.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:25.129
In addition, Iranian banks are increasingly
preparing their financial statements in line

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:30.949
with the international financial reporting
standards-means IFRS.

00:08:30.949 --> 00:08:42.220
We also attach high priority to enhancing
the AML/CFT framework through improvement

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:51.830
of KYC policies and procedures, to ensure
prevention of financial crime and facilitate

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:59.360
the reintegration of the Iranian banking system
into the global economy.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:08.440
Following the passage of the AML law and its
implementing regulations several years ago,

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:18.990
the recent enactment of the CFT law will remove
an important obstacle in the way of Iranian

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.880
banks' reengagement with their foreign counterparts.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:33.600
We remain committed to further strengthening
the AML/CFT framework, and we have requested

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:45.650
an IMF assessment against the FATF standards
and intend to join the Eurasian AML/CFT Group.

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:57.000
I am sure many of you are aware of the Iranian
economy's potential, with a population of

00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:08.350
about 80 million, a young and well-educated
workforce, and an entrepreneurial business

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:10.250
class.

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:19.790
The economy is also endowed with vast reserves
of oil and gas, ranks 18th globally in GDP

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.440
in PPP terms.

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:38.180
The $1.4 trillion Iranian economy is well-diversified
and enjoys a broad domestic industrial base.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:46.040
As cited elsewhere, Iran has the consumer
potential of Turkey, the oil reserves of Saudi

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:57.330
Arabia, the natural gas reserves of Russia,
and the mineral reserves of Australia.

00:10:57.330 --> 00:11:07.070
Over the past 25 years, the country has completed
five development plans and has attracted billions

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:16.780
of dollars of investment from over 50 countries,
primarily in the energy sector, petrochemical,

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:24.960
mining, steel industry, telecommunication,
car manufacturing, et cetera, and pharmaceuticals

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.150
industry.

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:38.890
Iran also has a large investment potential
in information technology and aviation industry.

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:47.040
So far, I have talked about, in a very brief
manner, the extensive opportunities that my

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:55.630
country offers to potential investors in different
business sectors of the country.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:12:03.660
Let me also give you a snapshot of what has
happened since three months ago, the date

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:09.920
of implementation of the JCPOA: almost nothing.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:19.670
On the JCPOA, EU and the United States are
committed to taking all administrative and

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:29.540
regulatory measures necessary to ensure successful
and effective lifting of sanctions to enable

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:34.670
Iran to reintegrate into the international
markets.

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:47.410
We are not discounting what has happened-what
has been done, such as OFAC's guidelines documents,

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:55.010
recent visits to other countries encouraging
the financial community working with us, general

00:12:55.010 --> 00:13:01.890
and specific license issued to encourage engagement
in trade and investment.

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:11.800
However, these have proved to be insufficient
after passage of three months.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:20.260
In general, we are not able to use our frozen
funds abroad.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:27.720
On this, serious efforts are made by our partners
to make the JCPOA work.

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:33.090
In my view, they have not honored their obligations.

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:44.250
If, according to our partners, it is our conduct
which prevents international banks engage

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:53.250
into business with us, they were fully aboard
of our conducts before signing the JCPOA.

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:56.020
We have not changed them.

00:13:56.020 --> 00:14:07.980
They signed it just for-just for us to deliver
our commitments and do nothing on the grounds

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:10.610
that Iran is responsible for.

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:12.950
This is not correct.

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:18.310
This is misstatement of facts.

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:27.310
That done, they need to do whatever is needed
to honor their commitments.

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:35.800
If it means more face-to-face contacts with
the international banks assuring them they

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:46.300
do not penalize them working in Iran, if it
means making changes to the laws and regulations

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:56.390
to give access to the U.S. financial systems-allow
U-turn, whatever is needed-they need to do.

00:14:56.390 --> 00:15:02.360
Otherwise, the JCPOA breaks up under its own
terms.

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:05.970
Thank you very much.

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:06.970
(Applause.)

00:15:06.970 --> 00:15:09.580
Sorry for-thank you.

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:14.100
ROSETT: Thank you very much.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:18.620
SEIF: Thank you very much.

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:21.330
ROSETT: Over there.

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:26.740
We will have a seat here.

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:28.550
Thank you.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:33.860
We should also just note this meeting is part
of the Peter McColough Series on International

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:36.660
Economics.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:38.100
Thank you kindly.

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:45.560
I'm going to ask a few questions, and then
we'll open it up.

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:50.910
And could I start by asking you just in brief,
was it sanctions that brought Iran to the

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:52.740
nuclear bargaining table?

00:15:52.740 --> 00:16:03.690
(Note: For the remainder of the transcript,
Mr. Seif's remarks are through an interpreter.)

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:12.130
SEIF: It seems that we have a kind of misunderstanding
here, and sometimes people have this misconception.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:21.200
Iran's economic performance, especially over
the past two years, clearly shows that Iran

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.370
was never in a bad economic condition.

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:27.340
And I think that these two are usually confused.

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:32.390
One is related to mismanagement we have had
in the past, and the other one is sanctions.

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:37.930
I don't deny the fact that sanctions have
had a cost for our economy.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:46.530
But the truth is, over the past two-and-a-half
years since President Rouhani has taken office,

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:52.410
the economic achievements we have had clearly
show that we have never been in a very bad

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:54.630
economic condition.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:17:00.140
Inflation was over 40 percent, but through
good management of resources right now is

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:02.610
less than 12 percent.

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:10.329
And when our economy was going through a recession,
right now we have 3 percent growth-it was

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:11.649
for last year.

00:17:11.649 --> 00:17:20.120
And these are some of the examples that show
that, due to our-we could have economic growth

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:22.059
even during sanctions.

00:17:22.059 --> 00:17:29.120
So one of the-when you look at the stability
in the market, especially in the foreign exchange

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:31.000
market, this is something we enjoy.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:34.420
And we have a very positive record over the
past two years.

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:39.169
And we see that volatility has dropped dramatically
and we have relative stability.

00:17:39.169 --> 00:17:42.580
ROSETT: Thank you.

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:48.259
And-pardon me, the echo effect.

00:17:48.259 --> 00:17:53.759
A question that I think would be of concern,
especially for Americans looking at potential

00:17:53.759 --> 00:18:03.299
business in any way related to Iran, for a
foreign investor in Iran today, to what extent

00:18:03.299 --> 00:18:10.460
would they be supporting or working with or
enriching the IRGC, the Islamic Revolutionary

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:13.679
Guard Corps, if they start to do business
with you?

00:18:13.679 --> 00:18:21.259
Is it actually possible to disentangle this
from the general economy?

00:18:21.259 --> 00:18:30.250
SEIF: It seems that here we have had some
exaggerations.

00:18:30.250 --> 00:18:35.970
Iran's economy is very transparent.

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:40.600
And the financial data is readily accessible.

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:42.679
And you can easily have access.

00:18:42.679 --> 00:18:49.669
I mean, you can go through KYC, know your
customer, and there are some services you

00:18:49.669 --> 00:18:55.009
can use, and any foreign investor can benefit
from those services for identification of

00:18:55.009 --> 00:18:56.250
the customers.

00:18:56.250 --> 00:19:03.629
So what you said is that foreign investment
should have the discretion and also the ability

00:19:03.629 --> 00:19:05.149
to know who he is dealing with.

00:19:05.149 --> 00:19:07.660
And this is something completely possible.

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:10.980
You can buy the services available inside
a country.

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:12.120
There are different firms.

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:14.250
There are credible firms.

00:19:14.250 --> 00:19:16.610
And they are providing know your customer
services.

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:22.669
Even for Iran, when we are trying to invest
in a foreign country, we-it could be a European

00:19:22.669 --> 00:19:28.870
country or a neighbor-we usually have know
your customer, and we try to use the local

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:31.230
services available in that country.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:35.731
So these companies and firms, they are providing
for that knowledge gap you have so you can

00:19:35.731 --> 00:19:36.899
make proper decisions.

00:19:36.899 --> 00:19:45.710
ROSETT: And should the IRGC have access to
U.S. dollars in the current discussion is-that's

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:47.480
all right.

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:52.860
The question is, do you think that the IRGC
and its businesses should have access to U.S.

00:19:52.860 --> 00:20:01.720
dollars?

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:06.010
The Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, do
you think they should have access to U.S.

00:20:06.010 --> 00:20:07.010
dollars?

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:13.269
In other words, if Iran gains access to U.S.
dollars, U-turn transactions and so on, should

00:20:13.269 --> 00:20:19.820
the IRGC be entitled to be part of that?

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:34.009
SEIF: What is-you see, the services that are
provided in the society can be used by anyway.

00:20:34.009 --> 00:20:38.440
And of course, you can get information-you
see what we are talking about would be about

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:46.669
the banks that would be helping us in converting
different currencies and to provide access

00:20:46.669 --> 00:20:53.240
to financial services of the U.S., so that
you convert to one foreign currency into another,

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:55.499
and settle a transaction.

00:20:55.499 --> 00:21:05.049
And these banks, they are obligated to provide
the services they have for their foreign partner,

00:21:05.049 --> 00:21:10.150
and to provide the assurance that-because,
remember, we are talking about know your customer

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:11.150
here.

00:21:11.150 --> 00:21:15.001
And you know that you also should know the
customer of your customers so that you can

00:21:15.001 --> 00:21:19.910
have complete coverage for the bank that is
providing the service.

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:31.450
ROSETT: Now that Iran and the P5+1 have agreed
that Iran will have a peaceful nuclear program,

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:37.340
could you tell us, in the question of how
Iran uses the resources that it has available,

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:44.990
which we're talking about, what is the cost
to Iran of the nuclear program, of the missile

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:47.559
program, of the military generally?

00:21:47.559 --> 00:21:49.170
If you could just break that out.

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:58.470
How much is Iran spending per year, however
you might sum it up?

00:21:58.470 --> 00:22:09.950
SEIF: Well, how much we are spending on diverse
type of activities is something that I cannot

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:12.159
provide you with any figure.

00:22:12.159 --> 00:22:19.460
But you know, every country makes a number
of decisions based on its priorities, and

00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:21.129
will invest accordingly.

00:22:21.129 --> 00:22:24.710
And these are, in fact, permissible activities.

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:30.710
And when it comes to peaceful nuclear activities,
we have the right-and also, this is the need

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:37.899
of every country, to provide a better future
for itself and to fully benefit from its potential

00:22:37.899 --> 00:22:40.720
resources and the talents it has domestically.

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:48.059
Fortunately, after many years, we could prove
that Iran has never tried to leave the peaceful

00:22:48.059 --> 00:22:55.059
nature of its nuclear program, and all the
activities have focused on those areas that

00:22:55.059 --> 00:22:59.580
are, let's say, peaceful and humanitarian.

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:06.710
So when it comes to the missile activities,
Iran, remember, is located in a very high-risk

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:08.639
part of the world.

00:23:08.639 --> 00:23:11.700
And you know, Iran's neighbors are not safe.

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:15.330
We have the growth of ISIS.

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:23.149
And those countries that have promoted ISIS
and its growth and to commit its atrocities-and

00:23:23.149 --> 00:23:27.879
I'm sure that you're fully aware of what ISIS
is doing to innocent civilians, to women,

00:23:27.879 --> 00:23:29.110
to children.

00:23:29.110 --> 00:23:30.470
These are the things you see.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:31.970
Who is responsible for that?

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:37.409
And the question is, how can Iran should get
a guarantee that it will not be the next target.

00:23:37.409 --> 00:23:41.559
And if Iran is a target of an invasion, who
is going to protect Iran?

00:23:41.559 --> 00:23:43.809
Who's protecting Yemen at the moment?

00:23:43.809 --> 00:23:49.670
The point here is based on experience, based
on our history, Iran has come to the conclusion

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:56.049
that we should rely on our own capabilities,
those capabilities that can be used for defensive

00:23:56.049 --> 00:24:00.049
purposes, defense against any kind of invasion.

00:24:00.049 --> 00:24:08.529
So I think that these are some natural developments
Iran is pursuing.

00:24:08.529 --> 00:24:12.539
But they have political interpretations, and
negative interpretations outside Iran.

00:24:12.539 --> 00:24:18.970
ROSETT: May I press you, as someone involved
in finance and money, simply to can you give

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:26.220
us a figure for how much Iran has invested
in its nuclear program?

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:29.740
What has it cost?

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:41.799
SEIF: Well, this question is something I don't
have the response to, but definitely we have

00:24:41.799 --> 00:24:44.139
done some investments.

00:24:44.139 --> 00:24:50.929
And of course the-it has been worth the effort,
any kind of investment done.

00:24:50.929 --> 00:24:56.730
First, you go through feasibility studies
and economic benefits of that investment,

00:24:56.730 --> 00:25:01.909
and whether it is compatible with the costs
or not.

00:25:01.909 --> 00:25:05.879
And Iran wanted to move in the right direction.

00:25:05.879 --> 00:25:08.179
But nobody cooperated with Iran.

00:25:08.179 --> 00:25:12.970
And Iran had to rely on its own domestic resources.

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:16.139
And that's why we went through the course
we went through.

00:25:16.139 --> 00:25:19.799
But unfortunately, it was surrounded by misunderstandings.

00:25:19.799 --> 00:25:25.070
And those people who have had those misrepresentations
of Iran's nuclear activities were themselves

00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:29.030
aware of the peaceful nature of Iran's nuclear
activities.

00:25:29.030 --> 00:25:33.369
But due to their political approach, they
wanted to create problems for Iran.

00:25:33.369 --> 00:25:41.169
ROSETT: During the period of intensive sanctions,
it was clear that some countries were helping

00:25:41.169 --> 00:25:44.139
Iran carry on.

00:25:44.139 --> 00:25:49.330
Was China the most helpful, or were there
other countries that were important players,

00:25:49.330 --> 00:25:52.299
such as Russia?

00:25:52.299 --> 00:26:02.570
SEIF: Well, I think that you want to know
which countries have had the biggest help

00:26:02.570 --> 00:26:03.570
provided?

00:26:03.570 --> 00:26:06.889
Well, Iran has had a lot of economic ties
with other countries.

00:26:06.889 --> 00:26:12.750
And there were countries that had positive
economic relations with Iran.

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:14.049
They continued to work with Iran.

00:26:14.049 --> 00:26:15.129
China was one of them.

00:26:15.129 --> 00:26:16.809
Russia was one of them.

00:26:16.809 --> 00:26:19.070
Some other countries also worked with Iran.

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:21.559
It doesn't change anything, you know.

00:26:21.559 --> 00:26:32.529
Iran under any circumstance, due to its economic
potentials, has the ability to run its economy

00:26:32.529 --> 00:26:39.799
without needing anyone, and not to become
dependent and sacrifice its values.

00:26:39.799 --> 00:26:45.700
ROSETT: I have two more questions I want to
try and get in before I open it up to our

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:47.110
members.

00:26:47.110 --> 00:26:48.909
So I'll just start.

00:26:48.909 --> 00:26:52.330
First is, this must be of concern to you.

00:26:52.330 --> 00:27:00.909
Iran, on the Transparency International Corruption
Perceptions Index ranks 130 out of 167 countries.

00:27:00.909 --> 00:27:05.570
It's worse than Russia or Pakistan.

00:27:05.570 --> 00:27:09.220
Just in brief, why is corruption such a problem?

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:17.200
And what are the chief obstacles for you in
trying to deal with it, to get rid of it?

00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:27.169
SEIF: I can give you the assurance that this
ranking itself is politically motivated.

00:27:27.169 --> 00:27:33.279
And Iran's domestic conditions-if you come
and live in Iran and see what kind of relations

00:27:33.279 --> 00:27:39.629
we have, you will realize that many countries
that have a higher ranking than Iran, or they

00:27:39.629 --> 00:27:44.510
have a better standing, they are in a worse
condition.

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:50.080
But due to the political nature of this approach,
Iran is ranked as what it is.

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:55.090
Over the past two years, we have had-based
on the reports we have provided we have made

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:57.480
some improvements.

00:27:57.480 --> 00:28:05.190
And I would like to ask you to pay attention
to the fact that Iran is being treated politically.

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:09.059
And sometimes you see that it will have negative
consequences.

00:28:09.059 --> 00:28:11.929
This is something we need to avoid.

00:28:11.929 --> 00:28:16.299
There are certain organizations that should
work based on facts and justice.

00:28:16.299 --> 00:28:20.850
And when you see that they have a biased perspective,
then you cannot trust their findings.

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:22.239
ROSETT: Thank you.

00:28:22.239 --> 00:28:26.270
And then finally, this is a question about
North Korea.

00:28:26.270 --> 00:28:30.440
Will sanctions stop North-you have a lot of
experience with how sanctions actually work

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:31.700
in practice.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:37.350
Will sanctions stop North Korea's nuclear
program?

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:51.169
SEIF: Well, regarding Iran, I told you that
it didn't have any impact on our viewpoints.

00:28:51.169 --> 00:28:55.210
And I told you that sanctions were costly
for our people.

00:28:55.210 --> 00:29:01.080
They increased the cost of transaction in
our country-10 to 15 percent increased cost

00:29:01.080 --> 00:29:03.340
of transaction.

00:29:03.340 --> 00:29:08.220
This is based on general research we conducted.

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:12.600
And you know that this itself can lead to
corruption.

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:19.529
And when you don't have a transparent banking
system, and it cannot provide the services

00:29:19.529 --> 00:29:28.380
needed for legitimate business practices,
then transactions will be diverted to a nontransparent

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:34.929
channel, through exchange bureaus or some
people who have some expertise in this kind

00:29:34.929 --> 00:29:40.470
of nontransparent types of transactions.

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:43.320
So sanctions, I think, will never work.

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:47.450
And I think that this is something which doesn't
belong to the modern times.

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:49.580
The world economy is changing.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:51.070
I don't know about North Korea.

00:29:51.070 --> 00:29:52.419
I don't know what's happening to it.

00:29:52.419 --> 00:29:59.779
But I think that if North Korea is acting
the way we were acting, or we have been acting,

00:29:59.779 --> 00:30:06.280
I'm sure that they can survive and I'm sure
that they can run their economy and they wouldn't

00:30:06.280 --> 00:30:10.080
be-I mean, sanctions will not a determining
effect.

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:11.989
Sanctions only put pressure on people.

00:30:11.989 --> 00:30:14.200
It doesn't change the course of policies.

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:19.980
But it only will impose certain costs and
create a context for non-transparency and

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:20.980
corruption.

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:22.029
ROSETT: Thank you, Governor.

00:30:22.029 --> 00:30:23.029
OK.

00:30:23.029 --> 00:30:26.080
I'd like to open this up to questions from
the audience.

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:28.520
A reminder, this meeting is on the record.

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:31.639
Please wait for the microphone, speak directly
into it.

00:30:31.639 --> 00:30:36.039
Let me start right here.

00:30:36.039 --> 00:30:37.980
Q: Thank you, Governor.

00:30:37.980 --> 00:30:40.450
I'm Hani Findakly with the Clinton Group.

00:30:40.450 --> 00:30:45.559
You've spoken about the diversity and richness
of the Iranian economy, and mentioned as an

00:30:45.559 --> 00:30:52.259
example the business in oil and natural gas,
you know, comparing them to Saudi Arabia and

00:30:52.259 --> 00:30:58.779
Russia, both of which are suffering, struggling
quite a bit because of the collapse of oil

00:30:58.779 --> 00:31:02.029
and gas prices, as well as minerals.

00:31:02.029 --> 00:31:10.289
So with this-with this situation at hand,
and the prospects for continued depressed

00:31:10.289 --> 00:31:17.450
energy prices, where do you see the future
of the Iranian economy, and what kind of vision

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:22.309
do you have in an environment that's changing
quite a bit, and especially with Iran consuming

00:31:22.309 --> 00:31:28.750
almost half of its oil production locally?

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:40.279
SEIF: It was a very good example you provided,
taking into account the vast oil and gas resources

00:31:40.279 --> 00:31:44.440
we have, and also the dependence we had on
oil revenues.

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:54.850
If you compare what we did when we were confronted
with the shock in oil prices, and compare

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:59.590
Iran to other countries-you see, we were the
least-effected country.

00:31:59.590 --> 00:32:01.590
It didn't change our inflation.

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:08.220
And this is exactly when we decreased inflation
from 40 percent to 12 percent.

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:15.799
So it didn't do anything to our inflation,
it didn't have an impact on our exchange rates.

00:32:15.799 --> 00:32:18.100
And we had our economic stability.

00:32:18.100 --> 00:32:20.789
We pushed forward with our economic plans.

00:32:20.789 --> 00:32:23.929
Compare us with other oil-rich countries.

00:32:23.929 --> 00:32:27.919
You see, our economy is stable.

00:32:27.919 --> 00:32:35.899
If in the past we had some economic mismanagement,
it should not be confused with the effects

00:32:35.899 --> 00:32:42.710
of sanctions, and taking into account the
policies we have had with respect to resilient

00:32:42.710 --> 00:32:47.440
economy, and also the measures we have taken
in this regard.

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:52.570
So this resiliency is something which is becoming
part of our economy.

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:58.600
And our country is very robust as a result
of the policies we have implemented, and also

00:32:58.600 --> 00:33:00.730
the diversity of our economy.

00:33:00.730 --> 00:33:07.549
So you see that we are running the economy,
and the objectives we have-we have had five

00:33:07.549 --> 00:33:08.850
development plans.

00:33:08.850 --> 00:33:13.299
The sixth one is being ratified by the Iranian
parliament.

00:33:13.299 --> 00:33:20.279
So the objectives we have, the long-term objectives,
the outlook we have for the next 20 years,

00:33:20.279 --> 00:33:28.760
and I think that we will be number-one economy,
and with a robust economy, in the region.

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:32.940
ROSETT: Go to the very back.

00:33:32.940 --> 00:33:37.590
Q: Thank you very much, Governor.

00:33:37.590 --> 00:33:40.200
I'd like to follow up on this question.

00:33:40.200 --> 00:33:43.440
You've answered a big part of it, but let's
put some numbers on it.

00:33:43.440 --> 00:33:49.619
Looking forward, if oil stays at $40 a barrel,
you have a program.

00:33:49.619 --> 00:33:56.649
What specifically might we see differently
if oil went up to, say, $50 or $55 a barrel

00:33:56.649 --> 00:33:57.940
by the end of the year?

00:33:57.940 --> 00:34:06.470
Would that-would we see any specific differences
in Iranian economic programs and investments?

00:34:06.470 --> 00:34:08.359
Thank you.

00:34:08.359 --> 00:34:12.810
SEIF: Thank you.

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:19.050
Definitely an increasing oil prices based
on the policies we have, and also plans we

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:29.100
have, will expedite Iran's economic development,
job creation, and also promotion of exports.

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:34.390
And of course, we have learned our lessons.

00:34:34.390 --> 00:34:42.160
And the excess income will not be spent on
import of consumer products and destruction

00:34:42.160 --> 00:34:43.160
of the economy.

00:34:43.160 --> 00:34:48.919
This is the main direction we have taken,
and definitely in the course of the implementation

00:34:48.919 --> 00:34:51.919
we are highly determined.

00:34:51.919 --> 00:34:56.230
ROSETT: Yeah.

00:34:56.230 --> 00:35:00.250
Q: Thank you.

00:35:00.250 --> 00:35:09.980
Robin Wright, with the U.S. Institute of Peace.

00:35:09.980 --> 00:35:12.580
Getting some major feedback here.

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:16.230
ROSETT: Tell you what, walk over there.

00:35:16.230 --> 00:35:18.340
Let's see if that works.

00:35:18.340 --> 00:35:20.220
SEIF: Maybe you have mobile.

00:35:20.220 --> 00:35:21.220
(Laughs.)

00:35:21.220 --> 00:35:24.680
Q: Let me ask you about your meeting yesterday
with Secretary of State (sic; Treasury) Jack

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:25.680
Lew.

00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:28.940
This is the first time you've had a bilateral
meeting with him.

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:32.550
Could you tell us about the nature of the
conversation and if you made any progress

00:35:32.550 --> 00:35:35.110
on your points, if you had any U.S. response?

00:35:35.110 --> 00:35:40.240
And secondly, looking at the bigger picture,
this is the third channel the United States

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:47.080
has developed after the diplomatic channel,
after the channel with Brett McGurk with the

00:35:47.080 --> 00:35:53.800
intelligence officers on the prisoner swap,
and now a channel through-with the Treasury.

00:35:53.800 --> 00:36:03.060
What does this reflect about the developing
dialogue between Tehran and Washington?

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:12.750
SEIF: With respect to what I've said, you
definitely know what I've been talking about.

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:23.760
And we don't talk about something specific
here unless we are talking about adherence

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:31.670
to the nuclear agreement, and also a commitment
to what we are obliged to implement.

00:36:31.670 --> 00:36:43.210
So it was our expectation that after the implementation
of JCPOA we could see Iran getting reconnected

00:36:43.210 --> 00:36:45.650
into the international economy.

00:36:45.650 --> 00:36:50.010
Unfortunately, the context doesn't show that.

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:56.010
And we are asking our partners in the discussions
and negotiations agreement to abide by their

00:36:56.010 --> 00:37:06.130
commitments so that we have tangible results
to present to our people.

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:16.820
(Technical difficulties; English audio gap.)

00:37:16.820 --> 00:37:23.660
SEIF: I don't call it a third channel.

00:37:23.660 --> 00:37:34.080
There was a need for a specific meeting related
to banking issues, and also the expectations

00:37:34.080 --> 00:37:40.380
we had regarding what each one is supposed
to do based on JCPOA.

00:37:40.380 --> 00:37:46.630
And we wanted to speed implementation of what
was promised.

00:37:46.630 --> 00:37:52.020
And I hope that these discussions would lead
to the result we have been expecting.

00:37:52.020 --> 00:37:54.670
ROSETT: Anyone on this side of the room?

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:56.210
(Laughter.)

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:59.290
Thank you.

00:37:59.290 --> 00:38:01.160
Q: Thank you.

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:03.870
Mohammed Khaishgi from The Resource Group.

00:38:03.870 --> 00:38:09.840
My question related to the sanctions, and
the continuing-the fact that several of the

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:14.890
sanctions require congressional approval of
the U.S., and the reality of the U.S. political

00:38:14.890 --> 00:38:15.890
system.

00:38:15.890 --> 00:38:21.690
What do you think is the outlook for the financial
sort of aspects of the nuclear accord, given

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:28.580
those political realities, and the fact that
there's likely to be some form of check on,

00:38:28.580 --> 00:38:35.010
I guess, Iran's ability to integrate the international
financial system going forward, and how you're

00:38:35.010 --> 00:38:37.020
going to navigate through that?

00:38:37.020 --> 00:38:39.440
Thank you.

00:38:39.440 --> 00:38:44.680
SEIF: I don't have any specific thoughts on
that.

00:38:44.680 --> 00:38:51.870
I think that the current U.S. administration
has accepted this commitment.

00:38:51.870 --> 00:38:58.590
And Iran is relying on this commitment.

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:05.390
And we expect that not only the U.S., but
also our European counterparts will abide

00:39:05.390 --> 00:39:08.250
by their commitments at any time.

00:39:08.250 --> 00:39:11.020
Otherwise, you can't talk about international
relations.

00:39:11.020 --> 00:39:16.100
That would-I mean, this is a kind of accepted
principle in international relations.

00:39:16.100 --> 00:39:22.710
So based on the expectations we have, the
commitments are accepted and we expect those

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:25.180
commitments to be fully respected.

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:29.130
Otherwise, that would be against the concept
of being committed.

00:39:29.130 --> 00:39:30.540
ROSETT: Oh, sorry.

00:39:30.540 --> 00:39:31.950
Q: Oh, sorry.

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:33.830
ROSETT: It's all right.

00:39:33.830 --> 00:39:34.830
Go ahead.

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:37.120
We'll get to you, yeah.

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:40.710
Q: Just a quick question to follow-up on one
of the questions here.

00:39:40.710 --> 00:39:43.920
You said several times-Margaret Warner, from
PBS.

00:39:43.920 --> 00:39:49.750
You have said several times that Iran is not
getting reconnected with the global economy,

00:39:49.750 --> 00:39:52.140
that the West isn't living up to its commitments.

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:53.300
Can you be more specific?

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:59.110
Do you think that the U.S. administration
is deliberately trying to frustrate Iran's

00:39:59.110 --> 00:40:00.340
efforts to reintegrate?

00:40:00.340 --> 00:40:07.410
Or is because Iran's financial system is so
out of step with the global standards that

00:40:07.410 --> 00:40:16.230
it's just very difficult for you to reintegrate,
as you'd like?

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:23.460
SEIF: What is important is the result of the
nuclear agreement, or JCPOA.

00:40:23.460 --> 00:40:29.480
If you look at the results, you can see whether
you have made any progress or not.

00:40:29.480 --> 00:40:37.320
What we see is that the impact we were expecting
to get is not what we see, at least on a tangible

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:38.990
basis.

00:40:38.990 --> 00:40:46.550
And for having normal banking relations, we
do not still have a normal condition.

00:40:46.550 --> 00:40:49.140
We are still in an abnormal condition.

00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:52.160
I don't want to say that no improvement has
been made.

00:40:52.160 --> 00:40:58.670
But I said in the opening remark that taking
into account the scope of the activities we

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:04.210
were supposed to engage in, and also the expectations
we had, nothing has happened.

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:11.560
And we hope that our-the other side of the
agreement will abide by its responsibilities.

00:41:11.560 --> 00:41:17.070
You see, from the Iranian side, we have fully
implemented what we were supposed to do-I

00:41:17.070 --> 00:41:18.270
mean, completely.

00:41:18.270 --> 00:41:22.190
And there is not even one single case that
you say that we did not go through.

00:41:22.190 --> 00:41:27.020
But the other part, has not acted on its commitments.

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:30.450
So that's what we're expecting, a speedy implementation
of those expectations.

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:34.320
ROSETT: I'm going to go to you and finish
off that row.

00:41:34.320 --> 00:41:35.540
Thank you.

00:41:35.540 --> 00:41:36.540
Q: Thank you.

00:41:36.540 --> 00:41:39.620
Barbara Slavin, from the Atlantic Council
and Al-Monitor.

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:44.050
Governor, good to see you again.

00:41:44.050 --> 00:41:50.540
What is the answer you get from major European
banks when you ask to restore correspondent

00:41:50.540 --> 00:41:52.360
relations with them?

00:41:52.360 --> 00:41:54.050
Why are they so hesitant?

00:41:54.050 --> 00:42:00.470
Do they tell you that they're afraid of new
sanctions coming down, uncertainty about the

00:42:00.470 --> 00:42:02.590
American political system?

00:42:02.590 --> 00:42:07.780
And how long do you think it'll take before
Iranians will be able to actually do banking

00:42:07.780 --> 00:42:10.110
with top-tier banks in Europe?

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:13.230
Thank you.

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:19.040
SEIF: I think we must make a distinction here.

00:42:19.040 --> 00:42:31.021
Remember, Iranian banks, whether they have
accepted international standards or not, whether

00:42:31.021 --> 00:42:33.620
they are up to the standards or not, this
is one issue.

00:42:33.620 --> 00:42:42.591
And the second question is why big European
banks or medium-sized European banks, they

00:42:42.591 --> 00:42:44.190
are hesitating.

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:49.350
Now, in the FATF list we have two countries.

00:42:49.350 --> 00:42:50.350
One is Iran.

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:52.340
The other one is North Korea.

00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:58.060
These are-in fact, these two countries are
in fact collaborating.

00:42:58.060 --> 00:43:02.680
Do you really think that Iran's economy is
similar to that of the North Korea?

00:43:02.680 --> 00:43:06.340
And many other countries-many-are not in that
list.

00:43:06.340 --> 00:43:10.320
Do you think that their condition is better
than Iranian banks?

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:13.600
We all know that this is not the case.

00:43:13.600 --> 00:43:17.590
And this is the political behavior Iran is
receiving.

00:43:17.590 --> 00:43:19.350
And you see it here also.

00:43:19.350 --> 00:43:29.480
So one of the reasons that we don't see a
speedy expansion of ties between Iran and

00:43:29.480 --> 00:43:33.880
international banks is FATF, which is wrong,
in the list.

00:43:33.880 --> 00:43:38.520
You know, last week my colleagues from the
Central Bank and the Ministry of Finance,

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:43.290
they had a presentation in FATF meeting in
Paris.

00:43:43.290 --> 00:43:46.130
And the participants were surprised.

00:43:46.130 --> 00:43:49.830
They couldn't imagine that we have taken so
many measures.

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:55.550
And when it comes, for example, to anti-money
laundering law was passed many years ago in

00:43:55.550 --> 00:43:57.600
our parliament.

00:43:57.600 --> 00:44:06.140
And before the nuclear agreement, we had made
proposals for combatting the financing of

00:44:06.140 --> 00:44:07.140
terrorism.

00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:11.230
It was presented to the parliament and last
month it was ratified by the parliament.

00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:19.900
And now our banks are supposed to comply with
these standards.

00:44:19.900 --> 00:44:25.710
But in an ideal world-well, it's not yet 100
percent compatible with international standards.

00:44:25.710 --> 00:44:28.410
But this is something natural.

00:44:28.410 --> 00:44:33.410
And of course, many of-and this is the case
for many countries across the globe.

00:44:33.410 --> 00:44:34.660
So I think this approach is wrong.

00:44:34.660 --> 00:44:43.180
The second issue is why European banks are
hesitating or maybe they don't have the courage

00:44:43.180 --> 00:44:49.240
to-maybe I should use the word-they don't
have the courage to work with Iranian banks,

00:44:49.240 --> 00:44:55.950
it's because of the heavy penalties that they
have been going through or impose upon them.

00:44:55.950 --> 00:44:59.310
And also, they have been asked not to work
with Iranian banks.

00:44:59.310 --> 00:45:00.600
And they are afraid.

00:45:00.600 --> 00:45:02.330
This is quite natural.

00:45:02.330 --> 00:45:11.630
If these threats are removed, that small weakness
related to lack of full compatibility with

00:45:11.630 --> 00:45:18.651
FATF standards would be immediately-I mean,
would be bridged in a very short time, and

00:45:18.651 --> 00:45:24.150
Iranian banks can reestablish their connections
with international banks.

00:45:24.150 --> 00:45:32.700
And the biggest European banks have had a
very good relationship with working with Iranian

00:45:32.700 --> 00:45:33.700
banks.

00:45:33.700 --> 00:45:35.850
I mean, there's a kind of positive history
we have.

00:45:35.850 --> 00:45:40.440
And that memory is still attractive to them.

00:45:40.440 --> 00:45:42.970
But this is ignored at the moment.

00:45:42.970 --> 00:45:49.020
And we want both sides of this agreement,
especially the U.S., to take the required

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:53.260
measures to remove the obstacles.

00:45:53.260 --> 00:45:58.990
Q: It was interesting to hear you catalogue
how much the Obama administration-

00:45:58.990 --> 00:46:00.420
ROSETT: Would you state your name and affiliation?

00:46:00.420 --> 00:46:01.500
Q: Reagan Thompson.

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:02.600
I'm from the Hill.

00:46:02.600 --> 00:46:05.930
And it's interesting to hear your catalogue
how much the Obama administration is doing

00:46:05.930 --> 00:46:08.290
to try and encourage foreign investment in
Iran.

00:46:08.290 --> 00:46:11.240
You mentioned OFAC guidelines, licenses, visits.

00:46:11.240 --> 00:46:18.250
And you also talked about how businesses can
access the very open financial data from Iran

00:46:18.250 --> 00:46:19.840
and can also know their customer.

00:46:19.840 --> 00:46:25.390
And as a result, they know of the IRGC's vast
influence in the Iranian economy.

00:46:25.390 --> 00:46:28.530
So my question is, you didn't talk about what
Iran is doing.

00:46:28.530 --> 00:46:32.020
There's always two parties involved in financial
transactions.

00:46:32.020 --> 00:46:37.080
What is Iran doing in terms of addressing
issues that many businesses are worried about,

00:46:37.080 --> 00:46:44.730
such as ballistic missile launches, the fact
that Iran remains a jurisdiction of money

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:48.760
laundering, and the fact that it's the world's
largest state sponsor of terror?

00:46:48.760 --> 00:46:54.920
So specific policies and then results of that
that your government is doing.

00:46:54.920 --> 00:47:05.150
SEIF: Well, I think that I have talked about
that before, and I think it's very clear.

00:47:05.150 --> 00:47:13.100
I mean, when you talk about Iran's missile
activities we only are benefitting from our

00:47:13.100 --> 00:47:18.990
previous experience, and we want to protect
our own citizens, and to reduce the threat

00:47:18.990 --> 00:47:20.580
of any aggression.

00:47:20.580 --> 00:47:22.640
This is a fact you need to recognize.

00:47:22.640 --> 00:47:30.870
And it seems that you have-you are fully informed
of ISIS activities in the region and also

00:47:30.870 --> 00:47:35.570
the types of crimes being committed by that
terrorist organization.

00:47:35.570 --> 00:47:42.420
Iran has this experience, and also we have
an experience of being the object of an aggression,

00:47:42.420 --> 00:47:44.490
and no one came to our help.

00:47:44.490 --> 00:47:51.870
And the first reaction was-we expected the
international community to respond, but no

00:47:51.870 --> 00:47:54.870
one responded when we were invaded by Iraq.

00:47:54.870 --> 00:48:02.910
So Iran has to depend on its own internal
capabilities, and not to provide any opportunity

00:48:02.910 --> 00:48:05.950
for any aggression against our country.

00:48:05.950 --> 00:48:07.900
So this is something normal.

00:48:07.900 --> 00:48:09.980
But I want to ask you this question.

00:48:09.980 --> 00:48:15.480
Over the course of the past few years, do
you have any history of Iran's aggression

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:19.190
against any of its neighbors?

00:48:19.190 --> 00:48:24.360
Why in an artificial way people are promoting
Iran-phobia?

00:48:24.360 --> 00:48:33.350
What Iran has done which is strange that has
become the object of so much media pressure?

00:48:33.350 --> 00:48:39.630
Iran's actions clearly indicates that we are
following on our own-following our principles,

00:48:39.630 --> 00:48:44.700
and we don't allow for any aggressor to commit
any aggression.

00:48:44.700 --> 00:48:49.230
And we are using all of the resources we have
to stop that, and we had eight years of an

00:48:49.230 --> 00:48:51.710
imposed war that we managed.

00:48:51.710 --> 00:48:59.350
And remember, we did not receive any foreign
assistance while our counterpart-I mean, the

00:48:59.350 --> 00:49:01.140
aggressor-was supported by every country.

00:49:01.140 --> 00:49:02.730
And see what has happened now.

00:49:02.730 --> 00:49:08.990
So this is experience we have had, and so
that's why we boosted our defense capabilities,

00:49:08.990 --> 00:49:14.630
and we are following down this path very carefully.

00:49:14.630 --> 00:49:23.530
And we always say that we don't have any other
intentions, save peaceful intentions.

00:49:23.530 --> 00:49:24.530
That's it.

00:49:24.530 --> 00:49:29.190
ROSETT: Would anyone like to ask a question
that answers his question?

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:30.190
OK.

00:49:30.190 --> 00:49:34.920
Yeah, in the way back, thank you.

00:49:34.920 --> 00:49:37.170
Q: Hi.

00:49:37.170 --> 00:49:38.180
My name is Valentina Pasquali.

00:49:38.180 --> 00:49:41.470
I am a reporter with ACAMS MoneyLaundering.com.

00:49:41.470 --> 00:49:45.260
You've mentioned the SFT law that was recently
passed.

00:49:45.260 --> 00:49:50.630
I was wondering if you'd give us a little
bit more details in terms of what it contains,

00:49:50.630 --> 00:49:56.660
and also tell us a bit more about other efforts
that are undergoing in Iran on the path to

00:49:56.660 --> 00:49:59.740
joining the Eurasian Group, which you've mentioned
at the beginning.

00:49:59.740 --> 00:50:03.540
Thank you.

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:14.320
SEIF: Over the past two-and-a-half years,
we have taken a number of measures to promote

00:50:14.320 --> 00:50:17.630
our banking sector standards.

00:50:17.630 --> 00:50:22.770
And most of them focus on more transparency,
increased transparency.

00:50:22.770 --> 00:50:31.340
One of the measures we have taken is obligating
banks when it come to the minimum disclosure

00:50:31.340 --> 00:50:38.450
requirements for our banks' financial statements
and balance sheets.

00:50:38.450 --> 00:50:44.390
So our banks need to disclose certain items
that are needed to be disclosed, and they

00:50:44.390 --> 00:50:48.340
can help in the KYC process.

00:50:48.340 --> 00:50:55.881
And as I pointed out, we have the anti-money-laundering
law being used in our banks, and our banks

00:50:55.881 --> 00:51:00.500
need to comply with that.

00:51:00.500 --> 00:51:10.300
The CFT was ratified last month, as I pointed
out, and this will be a preventive measure

00:51:10.300 --> 00:51:14.280
that will fight financing of terrorism.

00:51:14.280 --> 00:51:24.650
And this is very crucial for a country because
you know that our country is, in fact, surrounded

00:51:24.650 --> 00:51:29.370
by different terrorist activities.

00:51:29.370 --> 00:51:37.530
And based on our own priorities and to protect
our own safety, we needed to fight this, and

00:51:37.530 --> 00:51:40.470
we are compatible with the international standards.

00:51:40.470 --> 00:51:47.300
And when we explained what we have done to
FATF in Paris, as I said, the participants

00:51:47.300 --> 00:51:53.200
were surprised when they learned about the
progress and achievements we have had.

00:51:53.200 --> 00:52:02.040
And after this presentation and the presentation
we had, we are sure that the process for Iran

00:52:02.040 --> 00:52:06.070
to be taken out of that list would be facilitated.

00:52:06.070 --> 00:52:11.670
And I hope that those political considerations
I said will not act as a hindrance here.

00:52:11.670 --> 00:52:17.520
ROSETT: I think we have time for about two
more questions.

00:52:17.520 --> 00:52:23.750
I want to give this side of the room and-
Q: Thank you.

00:52:23.750 --> 00:52:28.010
Speaking for this side of the room, I'm John
Sullivan from George Mason.

00:52:28.010 --> 00:52:33.550
One of the things that we see quite a bit
in the media coverage of Iran, reporting coming

00:52:33.550 --> 00:52:39.860
out of presentations and speeches being made,
is the rather substantial reference to the

00:52:39.860 --> 00:52:45.800
resistance economy-the idea of resisting engagement
with the international economy.

00:52:45.800 --> 00:52:54.150
How do you reconcile that, plus the role played
by the IGRC, with the idea of creating an

00:52:54.150 --> 00:52:57.810
open-market, integrated economy to the rest
of the world?

00:52:57.810 --> 00:52:59.420
It does seem to be a contradiction.

00:52:59.420 --> 00:53:05.570
So explain it to us.

00:53:05.570 --> 00:53:16.050
SEIF: I think that there is a kind of serious
misunderstanding here, or maybe-let me explain

00:53:16.050 --> 00:53:19.340
this.

00:53:19.340 --> 00:53:28.510
When we talk about resilient economy, it goes
to creating a sense of stability in the economy

00:53:28.510 --> 00:53:40.730
and providing the context for dynamic growth,
and also avoiding any possible shock that

00:53:40.730 --> 00:53:49.690
might undermine the economy or present volatility
in the economy that would have an impact on

00:53:49.690 --> 00:53:51.670
the living standards of people.

00:53:51.670 --> 00:53:54.510
So that's our definition of resilient economy.

00:53:54.510 --> 00:54:02.770
So we rely on our domestic resources to create
jobs for productive purposes, for promotion

00:54:02.770 --> 00:54:11.510
of exports, and in no way-no one has this
interpretation that it is related to IRGC's

00:54:11.510 --> 00:54:14.760
activities or other types of activities.

00:54:14.760 --> 00:54:25.870
So the kind of plans or the plans we have
for different obligations and responsibilities

00:54:25.870 --> 00:54:32.720
of different parts of the government, we are
following on this agenda, and we are creating

00:54:32.720 --> 00:54:42.750
an economic condition where our economy would
be safe from different shocks and will-we

00:54:42.750 --> 00:54:48.550
would not-we will be able to reach the objectives
we have set for ourselves.

00:54:48.550 --> 00:54:56.900
As I said, you can compare the effect of the
oil-price shock on our economy and with that

00:54:56.900 --> 00:55:03.080
effect on other oil-exporting countries' economies,
and you see that our economy is resilient

00:55:03.080 --> 00:55:06.480
here when it comes to such shocks.

00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:13.520
And, you know, that drop in the price of oil
was a political measure.

00:55:13.520 --> 00:55:20.090
Our enemies, based on their political approach,
might try to target our weaknesses.

00:55:20.090 --> 00:55:26.110
And they thought that this is one of our weaknesses,
so if they drop the price of oil then our

00:55:26.110 --> 00:55:32.150
economy will collapse, and then we will not
be able to manage our economy.

00:55:32.150 --> 00:55:43.000
But you see what happened is that this economy
is perfectly stable and has been managed properly,

00:55:43.000 --> 00:55:47.820
and the economic objectives we have set for
ourselves, we could reach them.

00:55:47.820 --> 00:55:57.880
So we had decreasing pattern of inflation
drop, and our economic growth is continuing.

00:55:57.880 --> 00:56:04.331
I said that any increase in oil prices will
expedite our development and progress, but

00:56:04.331 --> 00:56:05.440
it cannot reverse it.

00:56:05.440 --> 00:56:07.090
That's a fact.

00:56:07.090 --> 00:56:10.280
ROSETT: OK, last question.

00:56:10.280 --> 00:56:12.670
The gentleman there.

00:56:12.670 --> 00:56:17.460
Oh, right there on this side.

00:56:17.460 --> 00:56:18.460
Yeah.

00:56:18.460 --> 00:56:19.850
Thank you.

00:56:19.850 --> 00:56:25.440
Q: Maz Yeremi (ph) with Goldman Sachs.

00:56:25.440 --> 00:56:27.830
(Continues through interpreter.)

00:56:27.830 --> 00:56:36.670
Well, if you think that Republicans win in
presidential elections and the new administration

00:56:36.670 --> 00:56:43.410
tears the agreement into pieces, based on
your agreement with European and Japan, which

00:56:43.410 --> 00:56:47.570
of these countries do you think that will
restore the sanctions?

00:56:47.570 --> 00:56:51.420
Or do they continue with normal economic relations?

00:56:51.420 --> 00:57:02.420
SEIF: So what is now evident is that, in international
contexts, we have demonstrated that we abide

00:57:02.420 --> 00:57:04.360
by our commitments.

00:57:04.360 --> 00:57:13.080
And our economic and business partners have
acknowledged the fact that working with Iran

00:57:13.080 --> 00:57:15.960
has never been a risk.

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:22.380
But whether another country interferes in
this and distorts the natural course of events,

00:57:22.380 --> 00:57:25.821
that would be in contradiction to international
regulations and laws.

00:57:25.821 --> 00:57:33.820
The U.S. administration has certain obligations
vis-�-vis Iran, and we expect the U.S. to

00:57:33.820 --> 00:57:37.890
respect these responsibilities and commitments.

00:57:37.890 --> 00:57:43.700
Whether a third country will-what will do
as a result of American interference is something

00:57:43.700 --> 00:57:45.540
that you should ask that country.

00:57:45.540 --> 00:57:53.300
And we expect our business partners to abide
by the contents of the agreements we signed,

00:57:53.300 --> 00:58:03.440
and to avoid any measure that will result
in a sense of mistrust in international contexts.

00:58:03.440 --> 00:58:08.590
ROSETT: And, with that, thank you very much
for your questions.

00:58:08.590 --> 00:58:10.780
Thank you, Governor Seif, for coming to speak
with us.

00:58:10.780 --> 00:58:12.080
And we conclude this meeting.

00:58:12.080 --> 00:58:13.080
Thank you.

00:58:13.080 --> 00:58:14.080
(Applause.)

00:58:14.080 --> 00:58:14.081
(END)

